---
/**
 * Button — token-driven action atom. Renders a real <a> when `href` is given (a link that
 * looks like a button) and a real <button> otherwise (Native HTML first, WORKFLOW.md §4).
 * Variants map to semantic colour roles only, so both brands theme it for free.
 *
 * Variants confirmed against Home's node.json (every `GIEVA Button` instance on the page):
 *   - `primary`   solid CTA fill, white label (Book Consultancy / Get Involved, Subscribe…)
 *   - `secondary` soft CTA tint + CTA border, CTA label (Take Test / Donate, Log In,
 *                 Learn more, Chat with us…) — used on both light and dark surfaces as-is,
 *                 so it does NOT hook into the inverse-surface colour flip.
 *   - `link`      no fill/border, just an underline-free CTA label + arrow (the
 *                 "Learn more" links inside the dark service/program cards).
 *   - `on-inverse` the base box with no fill and a white label — the CTA panel's row links
 *                 (Volunteer / Donate / Other Partnership Opportunities). Added for the NGO
 *                 About redesign, now used by `NgoCtaSection` on both NGO pages that carry that
 *                 panel; see the variant's CSS for why `link` doesn't fit.
 *
 * Colours consume the `--color-cta*` role, not `--color-accent-warm` directly: on Consultancy
 * the CTA colour and the warm accent coincide (both #E65320, so rendering is unchanged), but on
 * NGO they split (green CTAs vs orange eyebrows) — see tokens.css §3/§3b.
 * An arrow icon is the default, but not universal — some instances hide it (e.g. the Home
 * hero's `Book Consultancy` / `Take Test`, whose `ArrowRight` nodes are `visible: false`).
 * Pass `arrow={false}` to omit it; confirm per instance against the design.
 *
 * The arrow occupies a 20×20 box, not the size of the glyph. Every one of the 103 `ArrowRight`
 * nodes across every ingested frame is a 20×20 frame wrapping a 15.00×12.50 vector — the 2.5/3.75
 * of transparent margin is part of the component's metrics. Drawing it at 15×13 (as this did)
 * made every arrow-bearing button 5px narrower than the design: NGO's header measured 154.1/196.1
 * against 160/202, and the 11.8px shortfall in the actions group shifted the whole nav 6.4px
 * right of its designed position, since the pill distributes slack with space-between.
 *
 * The box was right but the glyph inside it was not. This used to draw a hand-approximated
 * STROKED arrow (`M11.2 4.5…`, stroke-width 1.6) spanning 13.4 × 11.0 — ~11% small in both axes
 * and ~28% heavier than the design, whose shaft is 1.25 thick. The design's arrow is a FILLED
 * glyph; the path below is the committed export verbatim
 * (design/figma/…/5990-3672/assets/icon-I5990-3705;5995-7314.svg) with `fill` swapped to
 * currentColor so the variants keep tinting it. Its bounds are x 2.5→17.5005 and y 3.75→16.2507
 * — exactly the 15.00 × 12.50 the node data reports, so the glyph now fills its box as designed.
 * Verified unchanged in the 2026-07-28 ingests, the newest design data we hold.
 *
 * HOVER. `primary` and `secondary` reveal their hover colour from the pointer's point of contact
 * via `HoverRipple` — read that component for the mechanism and docs/enhancement-backlog.md E-07
 * for the decision record. It is strictly an enhancement: with no JS the attribute stays inert
 * and the plain background swap below still runs. Default-on for those two variants rather than
 * opt-in per call site, because a page where some filled buttons sweep and their identical
 * neighbours snap reads as a bug, not as restraint — and per-call-site opt-in is exactly how
 * that drift happens. `ripple={false}` is the escape hatch if a placement ever needs it.
 * `secondary` also passes `ink`: its LABEL colour changes on hover, and a background circle
 * can't repaint text. `link` has no fill to grow, and `on-inverse`'s hover is a 10% white wash
 * that is too faint for the sweep to read, so neither takes it.
 *
 * One hand-drawn copy of this same glyph still lives outside this component (SiteFooter.astro).
 * The other two — ngo/about.astro and ngo/index.astro — were both the NGO CTA's row links, and
 * both went when that CTA became NgoCtaSection.astro and its rows became `on-inverse` Buttons.
 */
import HoverRipple from '@components/HoverRipple.astro';

interface Props {
  href?: string;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'link' | 'on-inverse';
  type?: 'button' | 'submit' | 'reset';
  arrow?: boolean;
  /** Escape hatch. The fill-bearing variants opt in automatically; see the note above. */
  ripple?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const {
  href,
  variant = 'primary',
  type = 'button',
  arrow = true,
  ripple = variant === 'primary' || variant === 'secondary',
  class: className,
} = Astro.props;
const cls = ['btn', `btn--${variant}`, className].filter(Boolean).join(' ');
const Tag = href ? 'a' : 'button';
/* `secondary` flips its label colour on hover as well as its fill, so it needs the ink layer;
   `primary` keeps a white label at both ends and only needs the fill. */
const ink = ripple && variant === 'secondary';

/* The glyph's provenance is in the doc comment above. It lives in a constant because the ink
   layer renders a second copy of the label row — the `<svg>` element itself is written out
   twice so Astro's scoped styles still reach it, but the 500-character path is not. */
const ARROW_PATH =
  'M17.3172 10.4425L11.6922 16.0675C11.5749 16.1848 11.4159 16.2507 11.25 16.2507C11.0841 16.2507 10.9251 16.1848 10.8078 16.0675C10.6905 15.9503 10.6247 15.7912 10.6247 15.6253C10.6247 15.4595 10.6905 15.3004 10.8078 15.1832L15.3664 10.6253H3.125C2.95924 10.6253 2.80027 10.5595 2.68306 10.4423C2.56585 10.3251 2.5 10.1661 2.5 10.0003C2.5 9.83459 2.56585 9.67562 2.68306 9.55841C2.80027 9.4412 2.95924 9.37535 3.125 9.37535H15.3664L10.8078 4.81754C10.6905 4.70026 10.6247 4.5412 10.6247 4.37535C10.6247 4.2095 10.6905 4.05044 10.8078 3.93316C10.9251 3.81588 11.0841 3.75 11.25 3.75C11.4159 3.75 11.5749 3.81588 11.6922 3.93316L17.3172 9.55816C17.3753 9.61621 17.4214 9.68514 17.4529 9.76101C17.4843 9.83688 17.5005 9.91821 17.5005 10.0003C17.5005 10.0825 17.4843 10.1638 17.4529 10.2397C17.4214 10.3156 17.3753 10.3845 17.3172 10.4425Z';
---

<Tag
  class={cls}
  href={href}
  type={href ? undefined : type}
  data-ripple={ripple ? '' : undefined}
>
  {
    ripple && (
      <HoverRipple ink={ink}>
        <span class="btn__label">
          <slot />
        </span>
        {arrow && (
          <svg
            class="btn__arrow"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            viewBox="0 0 20 20"
            fill="none"
            aria-hidden="true"
            focusable="false"
          >
            <path d={ARROW_PATH} fill="currentColor" />
          </svg>
        )}
      </HoverRipple>
    )
  }
  <span class="btn__label"><slot /></span>
  {
    arrow && (
      <svg
        class="btn__arrow"
        width="20"
        height="20"
        viewBox="0 0 20 20"
        fill="none"
        aria-hidden="true"
        focusable="false"
      >
        <path d={ARROW_PATH} fill="currentColor" />
      </svg>
    )
  }
</Tag>

<style>
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* 16px label→arrow gap: every `GIEVA Button` instance on Home carries itemSpacing 16 —
       solid, outline and link alike (audited across all 14) — so it lives on the base class. */
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    /* Design (GIEVA Button component): 32px horizontal padding (node 5963:3497/3503).
       Vertical is a brand token, not a constant: the declared Figma padding (16px) would give
       56px, but the instances measure 50px on Consultancy and 54px on NGO. --control-padding-block
       carries that split (12px / 14px) — see tokens.css. */
    padding: var(--control-padding-block) var(--space-lg);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-family-base);
    font-size: var(--type-body-lg-size);
    line-height: var(--type-body-lg-line);
    letter-spacing: var(--type-body-lg-tracking);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    transition:
      background-color var(--motion-fast) var(--easing-standard),
      color var(--motion-fast) var(--easing-standard),
      border-color var(--motion-fast) var(--easing-standard);
  }
  .btn__arrow {
    flex: none;
  }
  .btn--primary {
    background: var(--color-cta);
    border-color: var(--color-cta);
    color: var(--color-cta-contrast);
  }
  /* Guarded on `:not([data-ripple='on'])`: this whole-box swap is the baseline and stays the
     hover state until the script claims the button, at which point the circle takes over the
     fill and only the ring is left to handle (see "Hover reveal" below). */
  .btn--primary:not([data-ripple='on']):hover {
    background: var(--color-cta-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-cta-hover);
  }
  .btn--secondary {
    background: var(--color-cta-soft);
    border-color: var(--color-cta-soft-border);
    color: var(--color-cta);
  }
  .btn--secondary:not([data-ripple='on']):hover {
    background: var(--color-cta);
    border-color: var(--color-cta);
    color: var(--color-cta-contrast);
  }
  .btn--link {
    padding: 0;
    /* The base class reserves a transparent 1px border so the filled and outlined variants share
       a box; a bare text link has no outline to reserve for, and the 2px it adds shows up as a
       2px error where the design bottom-aligns the link inside a card. */
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    color: var(--color-cta);
    /* The link variant is its own Figma type style: `GIEVA.org/Arial/Regular underline` carries
       letterSpacing 0, where the plain `GIEVA.org/Arial/Regular` the base class uses carries
       −0.16. Confirmed on both brands (every "Learn more" / "View case study" label on both
       Home frames) and against the live variable definitions. */
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
  .btn--link .btn__label {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
  }
  .btn--link:hover {
    color: var(--color-cta-hover);
  }
  /* The link variant does NOT take the mobile tier's 18→14px drop. Every other button label
     shrinks with `--type-body-lg` (tokens.css §4), but the mobile frame keeps "Learn more" at
     18/24 — confirmed on the service card (node I12490:11429;5966:3579;5963:3494), where it sits
     beside 14px body copy in the same card, so the difference is deliberate rather than an
     un-updated instance. Restated here rather than excluded from the token, because at desktop
     the two roles genuinely do share a size. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .btn--link {
      font-size: var(--font-size-underline);
      line-height: var(--line-height-underline);
    }
  }
  /* `on-inverse` — the CTA panel's row links (NGO About node 12327:12146/12149/12152). A real
     `GIEVA Button` instance, so it keeps the base box verbatim (32px inline padding, 8px radius,
     16px label→arrow gap, the 20×20 arrow) and only drops the fill: the design gives these no
     background at all, just the white label of `GIEVA.org/Arial/Regular` over the teal panel.
     It is NOT `link` — that variant zeroes the padding and underlines, both wrong here.
     Hover is ours: the design ships no hover state for these, so a 10% white wash is inferred,
     chosen to read on the teal panel without introducing a colour the palette doesn't have. */
  .btn--on-inverse {
    background: transparent;
    border-color: transparent;
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
  }
  .btn--on-inverse:hover {
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.1);
  }

  /* ── Hover reveal ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The mechanism lives in HoverRipple; this is only the colour wiring. Each variant names the
     colour that grows, and — where the label changes too — the colour it grows underneath.

     Once the script has claimed the button (`data-ripple="on"`) the box keeps its RESTING fill
     and the circle carries the change; until then the plain `:hover` swaps above are still the
     hover state, so the no-JS baseline is the behaviour this replaced.

     The 1px border is the one thing the circle cannot reveal — `overflow` clips at the padding
     box, so the ring is always outside it. Leaving it at the resting colour drew a hard ring in
     the old colour around an already-swept button, so it cross-fades on the reveal's own
     duration and curve; at 1px that reads as part of the sweep rather than as a competing
     animation. */
  .btn--primary[data-ripple] {
    --ripple-fill: var(--color-cta-hover);
  }
  .btn--secondary[data-ripple] {
    --ripple-fill: var(--color-cta);
    --ripple-ink: var(--color-cta-contrast);
  }
  .btn[data-ripple='on'] {
    transition: border-color var(--ripple-out) var(--ripple-easing);
  }
  .btn[data-ripple='on']:hover {
    transition-duration: var(--ripple-in);
  }
  /* All that is left for the claimed state: the ring. The fill is the circle's job, and on
     `secondary` the label's job belongs to the ink copy, which flips on the same edge. */
  .btn--primary[data-ripple='on']:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-cta-hover);
  }
  .btn--secondary[data-ripple='on']:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-cta);
  }
</style>
